db/ref 17592186060965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060766 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060766 #edb/ref 1061 37890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060766 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5f0968c6-9ace-5735-9d92-61ff226b832e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060766 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060767 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060766 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060767 #edb/ref 1061 37913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060767 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3461449b-03e6-5f5d-aff8-7cec85835701" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060767 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060768 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060768 #edb/ref 1061 38032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060768 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "afbe0be3-1d3c-5408-9cda-36c61677f0fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060768 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060967 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060769 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060769 #edb/ref 1061 38686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060769 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e659ed87-b26a-54f6-bb76-e98cf99a6b01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060769 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060964 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we conduct a random sample of size $n=10$ from a normal population with the unknown population mean $\\mu$ and *known* population variance $\\sigma^2=9.$ After processing our results, we compute the mean of this sample using the usual methods and get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}=12\n$$\n\n \n\nThis is an unbiased estimate of the population mean $\\mu.$ However, it is unsatisfactory because we have no information regarding its reliability.\n\nFor this reason, it's often more helpful to give a range of possible values for $\\mu.$ In other words, instead of reporting a single estimate $\\overline{x},$ we might instead report a so-called **confidence interval,** which is an interval of the form\n\n$$\n(\\overline{x}-E, \\: \\overline{x}+E)\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overline{x}$ is our estimate of the population mean, and\n\n- $E$ is some **margin of error**.\n\nIn addition, we need to give some indication regarding the reliability of our confidence interval. With this in mind, we will construct a $\\boldsymbol{95\\%}$ **confidence interval** for the population mean $\\mu$. The precise meaning of this will become clear shortly." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060964 #edb/ref 1065 6232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060964 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f8ebcc7b-ddba-56d2-bb41-07916f3c2d09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060964 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060965 #edb/ref 1064 "Up to now, we've assumed that the underlying population is normally distributed. However, if we have a *sufficiently large* sample, then according to the central limit theorem, we can use the approximation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n} \\approx N(0,1).\n$$\n\nSo, even though we might not know the underlying distribution, we can still use our formula for the confidence interval of the population mean $\\mu{:}$\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060965 #edb/ref 1065 6245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060965 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4b9c6c26-d589-5847-a384-c431ad44a125" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060965 #edb/ref 1067 "Large Samples" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060966 #edb/ref 1064 "It's important to be able to interpret confidence intervals correctly.\n\nImagine we conduct several independent random samples from a population. The mean of the sample will be different for each sample. Some values of $\\overline{x}$ might lie close to the population mean $\\mu,$ and some could lie further away.\n\nSuppose we construct a $90\\%$ confidence interval for each sample and plot them on the same diagram. We might get a picture similar to the one below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc8dd707751cd9e1cb0da1bf618b6d7aed740fab875d8d49c7c89fc09b77321e.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, we see that nine out of ten of our confidence intervals contain $\\mu,$ while one of them (the red one) does *not* contain the population mean $\\mu.$ \n\nThe idea is that if we construct many $90\\%$ confidence intervals, one for each sample, then approximately $90\\%$ of these confidence intervals will contain the population mean $\\mu.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060966 #edb/ref 1065 6247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060966 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d71a924b-5f1e-5a89-a56b-d540a4a53d74" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060966 #edb/ref 1067 "Interpreting Confidence Intervals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060967 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population with the unknown population mean $\\mu$ and *known* population variance $\\sigma^2.$ Then, for a given value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ a $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(\\overline{x} - z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}, \\: \\overline{x} + z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1).$ \n\nThe endpoints of our confidence interval are called **confidence limits** and are given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nEach part of the formula above has a name:\n\n- $\\overline{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}$ is the standard error of the mean\n\n- $z_{\\alpha/2}$ is the corresponding $z$-score\n\n- $E = z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}$ is the margin of error\n\n![](images/58/581fc2da88e4419e78389a1b5eedb127f590641d0b7ee21c489ed85184795b13.svg)\n\nSo, our confidence interval can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\overset{―}{x}-[\\text{margin of error}], & \\,\\overset{―}{x}+[\\text{margin of error}]) \\\\ (\\overset{―}{x}-[\\text{[math]z[/math]-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}], & \\,\\overset{―}{x}+[\\text{[math]z[/math]-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}]) \\\\ (\\overset{―}{x}-{z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}, & \\,\\overset{―}{x}+{z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince computing a confidence interval amounts to finding the corresponding confidence limits, we will use the two terms interchangeably." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060967 #edb/ref 1065 6275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060967 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "cdae1125-df9d-525c-823d-64f129c3ab6f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060967 #edb/ref 1067 "Summarizing Confidence Intervals for Normal Populations With Known Population Variance" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060968 #edb/ref 1064 "In our example, the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is also normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\overline{X}\\sim N\\left(\\mu,\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right).\n$$\n\nBy transforming $\\overline{X}$ to a standard normal random variable, we have\n\n$$\nZ = \\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n}\\sim N(0,1).\n$$\n\nWe wish to find a $z$-interval that we're $95\\%$ confident that the random variable $Z$ lies within. This interval is indicated in the diagram below:\n\n![](images/50/50a0f49fe5c96ba3259832fbf1a0abcedffb342301ca069bb7b88d157a181019.png)\n\n  \n\nTo help with the notation, we define a new parameter $\\alpha.$ Since we're computing a $95\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ is precisely the area bounded by each \"tail\" that we're excluding from our confidence interval. Let's label the critical values at the endpoints of our interval as $\\pm z_{\\alpha/2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/3c/3cdfd2c72bf7fe65e5d1b584d46d4c846e74615e54c739a6d1ea990c95011a8a.png)\n\n  \n\nAccording to our diagram,\n\n$$\nP(Z > z_{0.025}) = P(Z < -z_{0.025}) = 0.025.\n$$\n\n  \n\nUsing a percentage points table for the standard normal distribution, we find that $z_{0.025} \\approx 1.96.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(Z > 1.96) = P(Z < -1.96) = 0.025.\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.96<Z<1.96)=0.95.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, there is a $95\\%$ probability that our random variable $Z$ will lie in the interval $(-1.96, 1.96).$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We solve the inequality inside the parentheses of the last probability statement for the population mean $\\mu\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1.96< & \\,Z<1.96 \\\\ -1.96< & \\,\\frac{\\overset{―}{x}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}<1.96 \\\\ -1.96\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}< & \\,\\overset{―}{x}-\\mu <1.96\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}} \\\\ -\\overset{―}{x}-1.96\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}< & \\,-\\mu \\,<-\\overset{―}{x}+1.96\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}} \\\\ \\overset{―}{x}+1.96\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}> & \\,\\mu >\\overset{―}{x}-1.96\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}} \\\\ \\overset{―}{x}-1.96\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}< & \\,\\mu <\\overset{―}{x}+1.96\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(\\overline{x} - 1.96\\cdot\\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}},\\: \\overline{x} + 1.96\\cdot\\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}}\\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting our values\n\n$$\nn=10, \\qquad\n\\overline{x}=12, \\qquad\n\\sigma=3,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that our $95\\%$ confidence interval for $\\mu$ is \n\n$$\n\\big( 12- 1.859, \\: 12 + 1.859 \\big).\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that we've seen how to construct a particular confidence interval, let's discuss the more general procedure." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060968 #edb/ref 1065 6398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060968 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "609f60a5-fbfd-5385-89e2-f80dd6281f39" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060968 #edb/ref 1067 "Constructing Confidence Intervals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498840 #edb/ref 1080 "22.1\\pm 6.393" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498840 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498840 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2f719a65-60e7-57dd-a27a-744f2bd0570e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498897 #edb/ref 1080 "$255\\pm 3.66$ words" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498897 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498897 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "48f20e6a-9608-55b0-904b-e2dd64d63ee1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498968 #edb/ref 1080 "$255\\pm 7.06$ words" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498968 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498968 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "688c9ce6-4b4e-5a38-a9b2-06226b46abd3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498983 #edb/ref 1080 "9.4\\pm 6.918" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498983 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186498983 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "710f42eb-828f-5104-9ba6-89eed5ec19de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499024 #edb/ref 1080 "12.7\\pm 9.430" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499024 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499024 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "82a41cab-338c-5c2e-9f93-261e0ef79794" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499068 #edb/ref 1080 "$36\\pm 3.66$ words" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499068 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499068 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9f796349-2ad0-566d-a3dd-4c798b32bcd2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499092 #edb/ref 1080 "9.4\\pm 4.930" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499092 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499092 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a62bc4bc-dfa0-54e7-b656-2ab71ae04f6c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499223 #edb/ref 1080 "$255\\pm 5.26$ words" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499223 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499223 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "de16784e-22c3-56ac-8038-f956c408ebf8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499235 #edb/ref 1080 "12.7\\pm 4.093" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499235 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499235 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e187b3cc-ab28-5083-8ad1-e0731cc94e98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499286 #edb/ref 1080 "$36\\pm 5.26$ words" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499286 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499286 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f8383bc3-1ee0-58af-8254-11d85b748de9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499385 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499385 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499385 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499385 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499385 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499385 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499385 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499385 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186499223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499385 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "90532975-7885-57b5-8831-bf9748a043d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499409 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499409 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186498983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499409 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499409 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499409 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186499235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499409 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499409 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499409 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186499235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499409 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cc2c8709-44f0-5048-b1ab-fe7e6a0dc83d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499443 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499443 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143023" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499443 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499443 #edb/ref 1071 "A primary school teacher records how many words his second-year students can read in 5 minutes. The number of words read is distributed with a population standard deviation of $\\sigma =36$ words. In a school of $180$ students, he finds that the mean number of read words is $255$ words. Find a $95%$ confidence interval for the mean number of words $\\mu $ for the entire population of second-year children.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499443 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "094a5111-7dbf-54d8-b6a9-b1a46e16163d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499443 #edb/ref 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=180\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=255,\\,n=180,\\,\\sigma =36.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/35/35037db1532092651e1974ea689b6004aa673c29720ac1d1c9d191c5929e9314.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.96:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}255\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\frac{36}{\\sqrt{180}} \\\\ 255\\pm 5.26\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499548 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499548 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142987" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499548 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499548 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/64/6486d3914bb0f7b4898220512e96630d13d2b0f4a5c1851938f98371a4c0255f.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=35$ from a normal population with standard deviation $\\sigma =9.4.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=12.7,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499548 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dd631a49-72c7-508a-98fa-29863f38f5bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499548 #edb/ref 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 12.7\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\frac{9.4}{\\sqrt{35}} \\\\  & 12.7\\pm 4.093\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514864 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186515657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514864 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186515662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514864 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186515666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514864 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186515695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514864 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186515698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514864 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514864 #edb/ref 1045 "Confidence Intervals for One Mean: Known Population Variance" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514864 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186515657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514864 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046644 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514864 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514864 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b23caed2-6738-5a67-8cf1-8f3f5f29cbd3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186514882 #edb/ref 1080 "15\\pm 0.613" #edb/ref