/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186515466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515558 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142501" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515558 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515558 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/64/6486d3914bb0f7b4898220512e96630d13d2b0f4a5c1851938f98371a4c0255f.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=441$  from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma =5.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=15,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515558 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6b59066d-5e7f-5521-a647-a4e84084b17c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515558 #edb/ref 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=441\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{441}} \\\\ 15\\pm 0.613\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515606 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186515503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515606 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142990" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515606 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515606 #edb/ref 1071 "A new weight-loss drug is being tested on a sample of $35$ patients. The monthly weight loss of a patient is normally distributed with standard deviation $\\sigma =13\\,\\text{lb}.$ During a particular month, the mean weight loss in the sample is $17.7\\,\\text{lb}.$ Find a $95%$ confidence interval for the mean monthly weight loss $\\mu $ of the entire population.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515606 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d4daf7f4-d328-5530-aaf2-46c1276ac650" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515606 #edb/ref 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=17.7\\,\\text{lb},\\,n=35,\\,\\sigma =13\\,\\text{lb}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.025}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.025})=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/35/35037db1532092651e1974ea689b6004aa673c29720ac1d1c9d191c5929e9314.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.025}=1.96:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}17.7\\pm 1.960\\cdot \\frac{13}{\\sqrt{35}} \\\\ 17.7\\pm 4.307\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515657 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186515662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515657 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5ae1c49f-ac9b-5137-80ce-bf2b191de428" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515657 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186515537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515662 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186515666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515662 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6b7f3bb3-b1ad-5d58-b892-d4f2e373d33c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515662 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186515558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515666 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186515698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515666 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8ab712a3-0c74-5547-b865-acd7fb2afd25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515666 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186499548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515695 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c5f43226-e606-5921-9ff9-fcffde1df948" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515695 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186499443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515698 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186515695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515698 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ce2405e8-18b7-5ace-94c6-778401fcddf3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186515698 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186515606 #edb/ref 1759218604541